Subject: JSON.parse compares an expected key of up to 14 bytes with one 16-byte compare

What this does
  - `JSONShapeCache.k16`: for each chain key that is plain and at most 14 bytes, its bytes, its closing quote, zeros, and its length in byte 15, written when the chain is extended (`plain[k]` is then 2)
  - the direct parser's expected-key test (0595) compares the input with that slot in one SSE2 compare and checks the first length + 1 lanes, when 16 input bytes remain; otherwise, and without SSE2, the byte comparison against the atom's string as before

Why we need it
  The expected-key test read each key's atom string header (scattered, often a cache miss) and called memcmp. json_app parse only: -2.5% to -3% cycles over two runs, -2.5% instructions.

Soundness
  Lanes 0..n of the slot are the key's n bytes and its quote, so a full match of those lanes is exactly the old test (bytes equal, then a quote). The load reads 16 input bytes only when at least 16 remain; the slot is 16 bytes. Slots are written with the chain's atoms and are only read for k < n, where plain[k] was set with them. Differentials identical old vs new: new jk16.js (5,040 inputs: warm chains with keys of 0-20 bytes, inputs with the key extended, shortened, last byte changed, escaped, padded, near the end of input and truncated), jrec, jslot, jdiff, jdiff2, jedge, jscan, jnum.

